---
description: Prefer utility classes with static methods over standalone helper functions
alwaysApply: true
---

# Helper functions → static utility classes

When extracting reusable helpers (mappers, validators, HTTP utilities, plugin builders, etc.), put them on a class as **static methods** — not as exported plain functions.

Match existing patterns such as `AuthUtils` in `libs/auth`.

## Do

```typescript
export class AuthUtils {
    static buildStripePlugin(opts: AuthOptions) {
        // ...
    }

    static mapAuthError(error: unknown): never {
        // ...
    }
}

// call site
AuthUtils.buildStripePlugin(opts);
```

## Don't

```typescript
export function buildStripePlugin(opts: AuthOptions) {
    // ...
}

export function mapAuthError(error: unknown): never {
    // ...
}
```

## Notes

- Name the class for the domain (`AuthUtils`, `BillingUtils`, `RepositoryUtils`), not a generic `Helpers`.
- Keep related helpers on the same class; split into a new class only when the domain diverges.
- Framework entrypoints that must be plain functions (e.g. Nest factories, Better Auth `createAuth`, React components) stay as functions — this rule applies to **helper/utility** logic those entrypoints call.
- Module-local one-liners used once in the same file may stay private; once shared or exported, use a static utility class.
